Z||000002||Patrick||08-16-99||09:23 PM||||<br>Kids, eh? <IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p>
Z||000003||lowCG||08-16-99||09:59 PM||||There was this guy back in high school who had a '67 Tempest with a 326,powerglide trans,we used to throw it in reverse at about 50mph,and smoke out the interior.<br>Never tried that in my Alfa,Austin Mini-Cooper,or even my Opel Ascona.<br>Ahhh,American cars.<br>Somehow I believe your Fiero deserves bettter than that kind of treatment,however.
Z||000004||theogre||08-16-99||10:48 PM||||I used to have a 73 maveric with a 289, no they weren't built that way the 289 was killed in 68,,,,,,, I didn't need to "power break" the car or any other such B.S...... All I needed do was hit the gas. With its very strong C4 tranny, and the 8" ring, the thing would basically sit there moving all of 6 feet at 50+ mph. (I had to hammer a small dent out of the pumpkin to get that rin in....... The C4 was carefully hand built from parts from 4 other trannys.) The best part was that with the tires I had, I didn't leave huge marks that anyone could bitch about. ((they used to make you clean off the marks if they cought you..... and fine you as well))<p>Would I do this with a fiero. #$%@$%@#$% No. Even the v6 can only do that if you abuse it. The maveric was specifically built to beat the crap out of. I wore out 3 motors in that car. (none of them altered from stock)
Z||000005||batboy||08-16-99||11:43 PM||||I was showing off years ago in front of a bunch of friends. One of them had a portable cassette recorder and wanted to tape me doing a mega burnout in my big block Chevelle. I started smoking those big ol' N-50 Mickey Thompson tires and suddenly all hell broke loose. I twisted a traction bar which caused one side of the rear end to start wheel hopping. Before I could shut it down, I tore up the ring and pinion gears in my 12 bolt posi 4.11 rear end and first gear in my prized Muncie close ratio 4 speed tranny. A rather expensive lesson in what can happen when you are showing off. That was the end of my burn-out days. Of course my buddies are probably still laughing at me. Made one heck of a recording though.
Z||000006||lowCG||08-17-99||01:08 AM||||Hey,Batboy,you'll like this one,we were standing around the driveway in true gearhead fashion,when some guy comes by in this halfway constructed,pro-street,big block '68 Camaro;he gets on it to try and show off,and the thing came on a serious "pipe"(cam,powerband,whatever),then the guy was tossing the wheel around like a ceasar's salad,fishtailing from ditch to ditch.<br>All this excitement on a street with kids walking,riding bikes.<br>He then idled home,presumably to hose out his shorts,and park the car before a lynch mob of angry parents were to form.
Z||000007||DJRice||08-17-99||07:03 AM||||When I was in high school there was a guy whose parents had just bought him one of those Dodge compact pickups (Dont recall the name).  This truck was in no way modified, but it did have the manual tranny.  OF course the first thing he did was rev the engine up, and pop the clutch.....<p>He hit the gym.  And ruined the tranny as I recall.<p>Hehe
Z||000008||Taijiguy||08-17-99||05:32 PM||||Hehee..good stories. I remember down in Virginia Beach one year, cruising on Atlantic Ave. pulling next to a guy in a tweaked Mustang (68 I think..sounded tough too) There I sat in a cherry Olds 442 convertable. This guy get's it in his mind to try and show some ass. He lights 'em up, and after about 3 seconds the rear u-joint gave up. This left the driveshaft to flail wildy under the car bouncing madly between the street and the undercarrage of his car till it finally shot out of the transmission tailpiece, leaving no seal for the trans fluid, which started to leak out all over the street. They guy finally got off it, and quietly coasted to the side of the road, totally humiliated by the presence of no less than a thousand witnesses, whom he certainly thought he'd impress with his display. We watched as his banged up driveshaft rolled across the street, coming to rest in the gutter. <br>The moral is, don't try to mess with a classic 442 ragtop, they have magical powers........or something like that.....
Z||000009||FieroMike||08-18-99||08:47 AM||||Usually I burn out a little going around a corner, or through some water or dirt.  Hardly ever on dry road, straight off the line... Hurts everything! <IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15>
Z||000010||87GT||08-18-99||09:11 PM||||When I first got my GT back in April, I wasnt quite use to the 5-Sp in it.  The 3rd day I had it, I pulled out and this truck was hauling tail at me, so I freaked.  I reved up way too much and let out the clutch.  I heard the tires raising hell and the car started side ways and all the sudden he caught grip, lined up right and hauled ass.  Scared the hell out of me, but later I thought it was cool.   Anyways, that is the only time I have lit my tires up.  Im not into that kind of thing. lol!
Z||000011||FieroGT||08-19-99||09:23 AM||||Last time I pulled a ripper from a standstill was about 4 years ago. I dumped the clutch at 3500 rpm and pulled a nice holeshot. 10 minutes later I noticed that the shifter (in my '85 SE-V6) was harder to row thru the gears. I pulled over and realized that I had torn both axle seals and trans oil was everywhere. I got off kind of lucky, 'cause I didn't damage anything else, unless you count the damage to my hands during the 3 hour axle seal installation! Never again. Bad roads inflict enough damage to my car(s), don't need any more help form me! I still like to get the high rps's once and a while. <IMG SRC="/ubb/wink.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15>
Z||000012||Pretender||08-20-99||03:36 AM||||i wish i had your problems!., this damn auto won't lite the tires up unless i have the tires hosed down in oil!..
